<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day07" xml:id="day-07-lecture">
    <title>Day 07</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>More Linear Regression</p>
  </introduction>


<objectives>
  <title>Covered in this class</title>
  <ul>
    <li><p>RSE, <m>R^2</m></p></li>
    <li><p>Confidence intervals and prediction intervals</p></li>
    <li><p>Qualitative predictors</p></li>
  </ul>
  
</objectives>








    <paragraphs xml:id="day07-continued-questions-to-ask">
      <title>inear Regression with Multiple Variables</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Fig3-4.png" >
              <shortdescription>
                Multiple linear regression model illustration.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>Predict <m>Y</m> on multiple variables <m>X</m></p>
                <p>
                  <md>
                  Y = \beta_0 + \beta_1X_1 + \beta_2X_2 + \cdots +
                  \beta_pX_p + \epsilon
                  </md>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Find good guesses for
                  <m>\hat{\beta}_0,\hat{\beta}_1,\ldots</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\hat{y}_i =
                  \hat{\beta}_0 + \hat{\beta}_1x_{i1} + \cdots +
                  \hat{\beta}_px_{ip}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p><m>e_i=y_i-\hat{y}_i</m> is the <m>i</m>th residual</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p><m>\operatorname{RSS}=\sum_i e_i^2</m></p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  RSS is minimized at
                  <em>least squares coefficient estimates</em>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="day07-review-questions">
        <title>Review: Questions to ask of your model</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is at least one of the predictors <m>X_1,\ldots,X_p</m>
                  useful in predicting the response?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Do all the predictors help to explain <m>Y</m>, or is only
                  a subset of the predictors useful?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Hypothesis test, F-statistic, at least one <m>\beta_j</m>
                  is non-zero
                </p>
              </li>
              <li><p>Forward and Backward selection</p></li>
              <li><p>RSE, <m>R^2</m></p></li>
              <li><p>Confidence intervals and prediction intervals</p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="question-3">
        <title>Question 3: How well does the model fit the data?</title>



        <p>
          Assessing the accuracy of the model
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
              <p>
                Residual standard error (RSE):
                <md>
                RSE=\sqrt{\frac{RSS}{n-p-1}}
                </md>
              </p>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li><p>Measure lack of fit.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
              <p>
                <m>R</m> squared:
                <md>
                R^2=\frac{TSS-RSS}{TSS}
                =1-\frac{RSS}{TSS}
                </md>
<md>
                TSS=\sum_i(y_i-\overline{y})^2
</md>
              </p>

            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Proportion in <m>[0,1]</m>; variability in <m>Y</m> that can be
                  explained using <m>X</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  0 means <m>Y</m> is not explained by linear regression on <m>X</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="r-squared-advertising">
        <title> <m>R^2</m> on Advertising data</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <ul>
              <li><p>Just TV: <m>R^2=0.61</m></p></li>
              <li><p>Just TV and radio: <m>R^2=0.89719</m></p></li>
              <li><p>All three variables: <m>R^2=0.8972</m></p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>R^2</m> always increases with more variables, even if those
                  variables are weakly associated to the response.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Adding another variable decreases RSS on training.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Small increase in <m>R^2</m> if we include newspaper with the
                  other two, so can safely ignore it.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Large increase going from just TV to TV and radio, so likely
                  want to keep radio.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="rse-advertising">
        <title>RSE on Advertising Data</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <ul>
              <li><p>Just TV: <m>RSE=3.26</m></p></li>
              <li><p>Just TV and radio: <m>RSE=1.681</m></p></li>
              <li><p>All three variables: <m>RSE=1.686</m></p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Similar argument about the big jump from TV to TV and radio,
                  but small difference after that.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Even though RSS went down, RSE goes up at the end because
                  the increase in <m>p</m> made a difference in
                  <m>RSE=\sqrt{\frac{RSS}{n-p-1}}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="look-at-the-data">
        <title>If all else fails, look at the data</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Fig3-5.png">
              <shortdescription>
                Residuals for the Advertising data.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Model overestimates sales for cases where either spent only
                  on TV, or only spent on radio.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Underestimates sales where budget was split between the two.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Implies there are some synergy (interaction effects)
                  between the two.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Later we will extend the model to allow for this interaction.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="question-4">
        <title>Question 4</title>


          <p>
            Given a set of predictor values, what response value should we
            predict, and how accurate is our prediction?
          </p>

      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="making-predictions">
        <title>Q4: Making predictions</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>
              Given estimates
              <m>\hat{\beta}_0,\ldots,\hat{\beta}_p</m> for
              <m>\beta_0,\ldots,\beta_p</m>.
            </p>

            <p>Least squares plane:</p>

            <p>
              <md>
              \hat{Y}=
              \hat{\beta}_0+\hat{\beta}_1X_1+\cdots+\hat{\beta}_pX_p
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>Estimate for the true population regression plane:</p>

            <p>
              <md>
              f(X)=
              \beta_0+\beta_1X_1+\cdots+\beta_pX_p
              </md>
            </p>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Fig3-5.png">
              <shortdescription>
                Advertising regression model.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="confidence-vs-prediction">
        <title>Confidence vs Prediction Model</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>
              Confidence Interval
            </p>

              <p>
                The range likely to contain the population parameter
                (mean, standard deviation) of interest.
              </p>

            <p component="slides-inst-notes">
              Shrink with more data <m>n</m>, converging to the population
              regression line/plane.
            </p>

            <p>
              Prediction Interval
            </p>

              <p>
                The range that likely contains the value of the dependent
                variable for a single new observation given specific values
                of the independent variables.
              </p>


            <p component="slides-inst-notes">
              Bounded by irreducible error; increasing <m>n</m> will not shrink it.
            </p>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Advertising-TV-With-ConfidenceInterval.png" >
              <shortdescription>
                Advertising TV regression with confidence interval.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="advertising-specific">
        <title>Specific to the Advertising Data</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>

              <p>Confidence interval</p>
              <p>
                Quantify the uncertainty surrounding the <em>average</em>
                sales over a large number of cities.
              </p>


            <p>
              Advertising example:
            </p>
              <p>
                If $100K is spent on TV, and $20K on radio,
                in each of <m>n</m> cities.
              </p>
              <p>
                95% CI for average sales:
                <m>[10,985,\,11,528]</m>.
              </p>


            <p component="slides-inst-notes">
              Interpretation: if we collect a large number of data sets like
              the Advertising data set, and construct a confidence interval
              for the average sales on the basis of each data set, given
              $100,000 in TV and $20,000 in radio advertising, then 95% of
              these confidence intervals will contain the true value of
              average sales.
            </p>
          </stack>

          <stack>

              <p>Prediction Interval</p>
              <p>
                Quantify the uncertainty in sales for a particular city.
              </p>



              <p>Advertising example:</p>
              <p>
                Given that $100,000 is spent on TV advertising and $20,000
                is spent on radio advertising in Gotham City.
              </p>
              <p>
                95% prediction interval for Gotham:
                <m>[7,930,\,14,580]</m>.
              </p>


            <p component="slides-inst-notes">
              We interpret this to mean that 95% of intervals of this form
              will contain the true value of <m>Y</m> for this city.
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="comparing-confidence-prediction">
        <title>Comparing the two</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li><p>Example showing the 1-d regression case.</p></li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Fixing TV = $150, confidence interval for sales is tiny.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  High probability the correct model takes a value in there.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li><p>On the other hand, prediction interval is large.</p></li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Need a wider band to be confident that a new observation
                  is contained in there.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Advertising-TV-With-ConfidenceInterval.png">
              <shortdescription>
                Advertising TV regression with confidence and prediction
                intervals.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs xml:id="review-questions-2">
        <title>Review: Questions to ask of your model</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Is at least one of the predictors <m>X_1,\ldots,X_p</m>
                  useful in predicting the response?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Do all the predictors help to explain <m>Y</m>, or is only
                  a subset of the predictors useful?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>How well does the model fit the data?</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Given a set of predictor values, what response value
                  should we predict, and how accurate is our prediction?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>


          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Hypothesis test, F-statistic, at least one <m>\beta_j</m>
                  is non-zero.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li><p>Forward and Backward selection.</p></li>
              <li><p>RSE, <m>R^2</m>, eyeball test.</p></li>
              <li><p>Confidence intervals and prediction intervals.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>


      

      <paragraphs xml:id="qualitative-vs-quantitative">
        <title>Qualitative Predictors</title>

        <p>
          Reminder: Qualitative vs Quantitative predictors
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>
              Quantitative:
            </p>


            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Anything with values in either integers or real numbers.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li><p>Temperature</p></li>
              <li><p>Year, even though integers</p></li>
              <li><p>Weight</p></li>
              <li><p>Number of cylinders in a car</p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <p>
             Qualitative/Categorical:
            </p>

            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li><p>Anything from an unordered set</p></li>
              <li><p>Country of origin</p></li>
              <li><p>Brand names</p></li>
              <li><p>Anything yes/no (married, has a cat)</p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="credit-card-balance">
        <title>New data set! Credit card balance</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Figure3-6.png" >
              <shortdescription>
                Credit card balance data.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p><c>own</c>: house ownership</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p><c>student</c>: student status</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p><c>status</c>: marital status</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p><c>region</c>: East, West, or South</p>
              </li>
            </ul>

            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <c>own</c> is referenced in the text, but isn't in any
                  of the online versions of the data set that I can find.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <c>region</c> is also referenced in the book, but not in
                  the online data sets.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="what-if">
        <title>What if....</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>

              <p>... your variables aren't quantitative?</p>


            <ul>
              <li><p>Home ownership</p></li>
              <li><p>Student status</p></li>
              <li><p>Major</p></li>
              <li><p>Gender</p></li>
              <li><p>Ethnicity</p></li>
              <li><p>Country of origin</p></li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>

              <p>Example</p>
              <p>
                Investigate differences in credit card balance between
                people who own a house and those who don't, ignoring the
                other variables.
              </p>

          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="one-hot-encoding">
        <title>One-hot encoding</title>


          <p>Create a new variable</p>

          <p>
            <md>
            x_i =
            \begin{cases}
            1 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person is a student}\\
            0 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person is not a student}
            \end{cases}
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>Model:</p>

          <p>
          <md>
            y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_i+\epsilon_i
            =
            \begin{cases}
            \beta_0+\beta_1+\epsilon_i &amp;
            \text{if <m>i</m>th person is student}\\
            \beta_0+\epsilon_i &amp;
            \text{if <m>i</m>th person isn't}
            \end{cases}
          </md>
          </p>

          <p>

        <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\beta_0</m> is average credit card balance among those who
              are not a student.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\beta_0+\beta_1</m> is the average for those who are students.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>\beta_1</m> is the average difference in balance.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="interpretation">
        <title>Interpretation</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Table3-7_ReplaceWithStudent.png" >
              <shortdescription>
                Credit card balance table comparing students and non-students.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>
          </stack>

          <stack>

              <p>Model:</p>
              <p>
                <md>
                y=480.36+396.46\cdot x_{\text{student}}
                </md>
              </p>

          </stack>
        </sidebyside>


        <p component="slides-inst-notes">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Average CC balance for non-students: $480.36.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Students have $396.46 additional debt, so $876.83.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="zero-one">
        <title>Who cares about 0/1?</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>
              Old version: 0/1
            </p>

            <p>
              <md>
              x_i =
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person is a student}\\
              0 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person is not a student}
              \end{cases}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>Model:</p>

            <p>
              <md>
              y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_i+\epsilon_i
              =
              \begin{cases}
              \beta_0+\beta_1+\epsilon_i &amp;
              \text{if <m>i</m>th person is student}\\
              \beta_0+\epsilon_i &amp;
              \text{if <m>i</m>th person isn't}
              \end{cases}
              </md>
            </p>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <p>
              Alternative version: <m>\pm1</m>
            </p>

            <p>
              <md>
              x_i =
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person is a student}\\
              -1 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person is not a student}
              \end{cases}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>Model:</p>

            <p>
              <md>
              y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_i+\epsilon_i
              =
              \begin{cases}
              \beta_0+\beta_1+\epsilon_i &amp;
              \text{if <m>i</m>th person is student}\\
              \beta_0-\beta_1+\epsilon_i &amp;
              \text{if <m>i</m>th person isn't}
              \end{cases}
              </md>
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p component="slides-inst-notes">
          Note that the predictions will be the same.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="more-than-two-levels">
        <title>Qualitative Predictor with More than Two Levels</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>



     <p>
            <table>
              <title>Region</title>
              
              
              <tabular>
                <row>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell><m>x_{i1}</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x_{i2}</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>South</cell>
                  <cell>1</cell>
                  <cell>0</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>West</cell>
                  <cell>0</cell>
                  <cell>1</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell>East</cell>
                  <cell>0</cell>
                  <cell>0</cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            </table>
          </p>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            
              <p>Create spare dummy variables:</p>


            <p>
              <md>
              x_{i1}=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person from South}\\
              0 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person not from South}
              \end{cases}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              <md>
              x_{i2}=
              \begin{cases}
              1 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person from West}\\
              0 &amp; \text{if <m>i</m>th person not from West}
              \end{cases}
              </md>
            </p>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
          <md>
          y_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\epsilon_i
          =
          \begin{cases}
          \beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if <m>i</m>th person from South}\\
          \beta_0+\beta_2x_{i2}+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if <m>i</m>th person from West}\\
          \beta_0+\epsilon_i &amp;
          \text{if <m>i</m>th person from East}
          \end{cases}
          </md>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="more-on-multiple-levels">
        <title>More on multiple levels</title>

        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li><p>Always one less variable than levels.</p></li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Level with no dummy variable is called the baseline.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>

          <stack>
            <image source="figures/Table3-8.png" >
              <shortdescription>
                Regression results for regional dummy variables.
              </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <ul component="slides-inst-notes">
              <li>
                <p>Balance for the baseline (EAST) is $531.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>South has $18.69 less than east.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>West has $12.50 less than east.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>p</m>-values for dummy variables are large, so can't reject
                  null hypothesis.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  No statistical evidence that there's a difference in CC
                  balance in different regions.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </paragraphs>


  </handout>
